# Guitar chord vocabulary

The shared engine is `src/chords/guitarVoicings.js`. Both layouts consume the same
catalog and position maps; no platform UI or state changes are required.

Selection uses existing open/E/A/CAGED grips plus compact movable guitar grips.
It never distributes individual chord tones over strings. Unsupported extensions
return no candidates rather than silently substituting a major/minor triad.
Every supported quality/extension is tested in all 12 keys with multiple positions.

`theoreticalIntervals` is copied from chordTheory and never reduced. A voicing
separately records `playedIntervals`, `omittedIntervals`, `omissions`, and
`doubledIntervals`. Default representatives retain roots, thirds, altered fifths,
suspensions, sevenths and named extensions. Only the perfect fifth may be omitted
by default. Explicit omit3 grips can be assessed with an allowed-omission policy,
but are not silently used as major/minor representatives.

Candidates must pass harmonic checks before ergonomics: at least three sounding
strings, no foreign tones, at most four fingers, no barre crossing a sounding open
or lower fret, and at most three frets between the lowest/highest pressed note.
Selection is lexicographic, not a weighted ergonomic contest:

1. Validated built-in dictionary representatives.
2. Open and basic movable families listed for each quality/extension in
   `voicingSelection.js`.
3. Other CAGED positions.
4. Special upper-string, high-register, and omitted-tone alternatives.

Open status, a first-position band (lowest pressed fret through fret 5), editorial
family order, and position resolve ties before ergonomic score. The band only
classifies candidates; it never sets the viewport or pins a root to a fret.
An easy upper-register grip cannot displace an available basic representative.
The editorial family order is explicit product vocabulary, not a measured claim
that every guitar dictionary agrees on one universally most popular grip.

Ergonomic tie-breaking accounts for fingers, barres, physical fret distance
(648mm reference scale length), jumps and muting. Open strings do not inflate
hand span. These are conservative heuristics, not biomechanical proof for every
hand. Interior muted strings require controlled strumming/fingerpicking.

The built-in catalog is validated before lookup maps are made, retaining legacy
IDs when a rejected grip is replaced. Position maps use the same selection tuple
as the catalog. Manually authored accompaniment voicings and
the theoretical all-notes fretboard remain independent.

Chord labels share `getChordToneLabel` in chordTheory. Canonical pitch identities
remain unchanged for comparison/audio; displayed letters come from each tone's
diatonic degree relative to the displayed root. This gives B–D#–F#–C# for Badd9,
Db–F–Ab for Db major, and E# in F#maj7. Catalog cards, selected positions, and
progression boards all use this spelling rather than global sharp/flat swaps.

Vocabulary references (consulted September 2026):

- [Guitar Prof: ninth omit5 grips](https://www.guitarprof.it/2021/01/20/accordo-di-nona-omit-5-voicing-costruzione-diteggiature/)
- [Jazz Guitar Licks: major ninth vocabulary](https://www.jazz-guitar-licks.com/pages/chords/major-9th-guitar-chord-diagrams-drop-2-voicings.html)
- [Badd9 upper-string grip](https://guitartoolhub.com/chords/b-add9-chord-guitar)
- [Standard Guitar Badd9 variants and spelled chord tones](https://www.standardguitar.com/b-added-9th-guitar-chord)

Fret data is maintained as compact root-relative patterns and independently
checked against standard tuning; article text and illustrations are not copied.
